# Constitutional Acts and Reforms in British India

> From the Regulating Act of 1773 to the Indian Independence Act of 1947, a
> chain of British laws slowly shaped India's government, and our own
> Constitution borrowed from several of them. SSC, RRB and police papers ask
> which Act created which office, which one began dyarchy, and which one
> introduced separate electorates. Learn the chain in order and the answers
> follow.

## 1. Company Rule: 1773 to 1853

| Act | Year | Main provisions |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Regulating Act** | 1773 | **Governor-General of Bengal** (Warren Hastings) with a council of four; **Supreme Court at Calcutta** (1774); first step to parliamentary control of the Company |
| **Pitt's India Act** | 1784 | **Board of Control** in London for political affairs; Court of Directors kept commercial affairs ("dual control") |
| **Charter Act** | 1793 | Renewed the Company's charter for 20 years |
| **Charter Act** | 1813 | Ended the Company's **trade monopoly** in India (tea and China trade excepted); allowed missionaries; set aside Rs 1 lakh yearly for education |
| **Charter Act** | 1833 | **Governor-General of India** (Bentinck); ended the Company's commercial role; added a **law member** (Macaulay); Indians not to be barred from office by birth or religion |
| **Charter Act** | 1853 | Last Charter Act; separate legislative wing for the Governor-General's council; **open competition** for the civil service |

- The 1773 Act also barred Company servants from private trade and
  accepting gifts.
- The 1833 Act made the Governor-General's government the single legislative
  authority for all British India, ending the separate lawmaking powers of
  Bombay and Madras.
- The 1853 Act led to the Macaulay Committee (1854) and a competitive exam for
  the Indian Civil Service.

## 2. Crown Rule: 1858 to 1909

| Act | Year | Main provisions |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Government of India Act (Act for the Better Government of India)** | 1858 | Ended Company rule; **Secretary of State for India** with a **Council of India** of 15 members; Governor-General became **Viceroy**; Queen's Proclamation of 1 November 1858 |
| **Indian Councils Act** | 1861 | **Portfolio system** (Canning); non-official Indians nominated to the legislative council; restored lawmaking powers to Bombay and Madras; Viceroy given power to issue ordinances |
| **Indian Councils Act** | 1892 | Larger councils; **indirect election** through nomination on recommendation; right to discuss the budget and put questions |
| **Indian Councils Act (Morley-Minto Reforms)** | 1909 | **Separate electorates for Muslims**; elected members raised; **Satyendra Prasanno Sinha** became first Indian in the Viceroy's Executive Council |

[definition]
The **portfolio system** (1861) divided the Viceroy's council into departments,
each led by a member, which is the root of the later Cabinet-style government.
[/definition]

- The first Indians nominated in 1861 were the **Raja of Benares**, the
  **Maharaja of Patiala** and **Sir Dinkar Rao**.
- The 1909 Act is called the **Morley-Minto Reforms** after **John Morley**,
  Secretary of State, and **Lord Minto**, Viceroy. **Minto** is described as the
  father of communal electorates.
- The Act of 1892 did not use the word "election", but it introduced the idea
  through nomination.

## 3. Government of India Act 1919: Dyarchy

| Feature | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Reform known as | **Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms** (Edwin Montagu, Secretary of State; Lord Chelmsford, Viceroy) |
| Announcement | **Montagu Declaration, August 1917**: gradual development of self-governing institutions and responsible government |
| **Dyarchy** | In the provinces, subjects split into **reserved** (Governor and executive council) and **transferred** (Indian ministers answerable to the legislature) |
| Centre | Bicameral legislature: **Council of State** and **Legislative Assembly** |
| Electorates | Separate electorates extended to Sikhs, Indian Christians, Anglo-Indians and Europeans |
| Other | Office of **High Commissioner for India** in London; a **Public Service Commission** (set up in 1926); provision for a statutory commission after ten years |

- The Act came into force in **1921**, and dyarchy was in the provinces, not
  the Centre.
- The ten-year review clause produced the **Simon Commission** in 1927, two
  years earlier than the schedule, which Indians boycotted as an all-white body.
- The Congress at its special session in 1918 called the proposals
  "disappointing and unsatisfactory".

## 4. Government of India Act 1935

[definition]
**Provincial autonomy** means that elected ministers in the provinces, not the
Governor and his nominees, run all provincial subjects. The Act of 1935
introduced it in place of provincial dyarchy.
[/definition]

- **Proposed All-India Federation** of British Indian provinces and princely
  states, with a federal legislature. It never began, because the princely
  states did not agree to join.
- **Dyarchy at the Centre**: defence and foreign affairs were to stay under
  the Governor-General, with other subjects for ministers, but this depended on
  the federation and so never came into operation.
- **Three lists** of subjects: Federal, Provincial and Concurrent, which
  later shaped the Seventh Schedule of India's Constitution.
- **Provincial autonomy** came into effect in **1937** after elections.
- **Federal Court** (1937), the **Reserve Bank of India** (established 1935)
  and provincial and federal **Public Service Commissions**.
- **Sindh** separated from Bombay, **Orissa** and **Bihar** were separated, and
  **Burma** was separated from India (1937).
- Separate electorates continued and were extended to the depressed classes
  after the **Communal Award (1932)** and the Poona Pact.
- Voters grew from about five million to about thirty-five million.

The 1935 Act is the main source of the Constitution of India's federal
structure, the office of Governor and the emergency provisions. It is a
long Act, one of the longest passed by the British Parliament for India.

## 5. Indian Independence Act 1947 and Quick Map

- The Act received royal assent on **18 July 1947**.
- It created two independent dominions, **India and Pakistan**, from **15
  August 1947**, ended British paramountcy over princely states, and made the
  Constituent Assemblies the legislatures of the new dominions.
- Governor-General of each dominion became a nominal head.

| Question word | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Governor-General of Bengal | Regulating Act 1773 |
| Board of Control | Pitt's India Act 1784 |
| Governor-General of India | Charter Act 1833 |
| Open competition for civil service | Charter Act 1853 |
| Secretary of State for India | Act of 1858 |
| Portfolio system | Indian Councils Act 1861 |
| Separate electorates (Muslims) | 1909 Act |
| Dyarchy in provinces | 1919 Act |
| Provincial autonomy, Federal Court, RBI | 1935 Act |
| Partition and dominions | 1947 Act |

[tip]
Memory trick: "Regulating, Pitt, Charters (1793, 1813, 1833, 1853), Crown 1858,
Councils 1861, 1892, 1909, Government 1919, 1935, Independence 1947."
[/tip]

[example]
Question: "Which Act introduced dyarchy in the provinces?" Dyarchy means two
rulers: the reserved subjects stayed with the Governor, and transferred
subjects passed to ministers. That arrangement came with the Montagu-Chelmsford
reforms, so the answer is the Government of India Act, 1919, not 1935. The
1935 Act replaced it with provincial autonomy.
[/example]

[summary]
The Regulating Act (1773) created the Governor-General of Bengal, Pitt's Act
(1784) the Board of Control, the 1833 Charter Act the Governor-General of India
and the 1853 Charter Act open competition for the civil service. After 1857 the
Act of 1858 brought the Crown and a Secretary of State. The Councils Acts of
1861, 1892 and 1909 widened participation, the last adding separate
electorates for Muslims. The 1919 Act brought dyarchy in the provinces, the
1935 Act provincial autonomy, the Federal Court and the RBI, and the
Independence Act of 1947 ended British rule and created two dominions.
[/summary]
